              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#history"} History
          %h2#what_should_a_set_be What should a set be?
          %p
            The concept of
            %em set
            appears in several different guises in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            It is common to use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            in which ‘set’ is an undefined term; this is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            as a foundation. In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural approach
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+set"} structured set
            in some way or another.
          %p
            Even if sets are not at the bottom of your foundations, still they are probably close. For instance, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            sometimes sets are defined as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated+object"} 0-truncated
            homotopy types; see below.
          %p
            Material set theory conflates two notions of sets, which were elegantly (but not first) described by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathieu+Dupont"} Mathieu Dupont
            in
            %a{:href => "http://math.breckes.org/2009/03/the-two-meanings-of-the-word-%E2%80%9Cset%E2%80%9D-in-mathematics/"} a blog post
            as ‘set¹’ and ‘set²’, which we will here call ‘abstract set’ and ‘concrete set’. In the latter case (set²), we have some fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+of+discourse"} universe of discourse
            (say, consisting of all real numbers), and a
            %strong concrete set
            is a set of elements of this universe (so a set of real numbers, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 5
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{5\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x | x \gt 2 \}
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
            %strong abstract set
            is a purely abstract concept, an unstructured (except perhaps for the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality+relation"} equality relation
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Lawvere
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Cantor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            (Kardinalzahl) was the abstraction from a concrete set to its underlying abstract set.
          %p
            Here we adopt a structural approach, in which a
            %strong set
            is an abstract set, a (mostly) unstructured
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/collection"} collection
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small
            subset of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            comes with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} isomorphism
            to its class of small subsets.
          %h2#what_is_a_set What is a set?
          %p We still need to clarify exactly what sort of collection a set is. Although most foundations leave this unspecified, we may (perhaps circularly, and perhaps controversially) define it in various ways, as follows:
          %h3#in_category_theory In category theory
          #AsCategory.un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong set
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              (or instead an
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} groupoid
              or even an
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-category"} category
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+category"} skeletal
          %p Each of these three adjectives describes a different aspect of what makes something a ‘set’, and they serve different purposes, which should not be conflated.
          %p
            That a set is (in the category-theoretic sense)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete
            is the most important point; nobody would call a category a ‘set’ merely because it is small and skeletal. (This clause is also the reason why it makes no difference whether we start from categories,
            = succeed "-groupois," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-categories;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-groupoid"} groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            (and the fact that isomorphism is an
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
            as elements of the set.
          %p
            That a set is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            allows there to be a collection of ‘all’ sets; this collection is not itself small, but we still have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large category
            of all sets,
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            instead. But notice that the difference between a set and a class is a rather technical one; a class is just like a set, only (possibly) larger. Indeed, not everyone would agree with the requirement that a set must be small; although that is probably the most common way of talking to deal with size issues, it is not the only way. Another way, used by no less an authority than
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+Work"} Categories Work
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inaccessible+cardinal"} inaccessible cardinal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            and define a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small set
            to be a set whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            is less than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p
            That a set is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+category"} skeletal
            is arguably the least important requirement; in fact, it violates the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoid"} setoid
            instead. However, if you forbid yourself from referring to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            of elements of the setoid (which are the objects of the small discrete category) — or, equivalently, interpret “equality” to mean “isomorphism” therein — then you cannot distinguish the setoid from a set; each is merely a (small) collection of elements with an equivalence relation (called ‘equivalence’ in the setoid and ‘equality’ in the set, in both cases corresponding to isomorphism in a small discrete category). While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/size+issues"} size issues
            are real and cannot be ignored completely, it is possible to adopt foundations in which the issue of skeletality simply does not appear.
          %h3#InHoTT In homotopy type theory
          %p
            As
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            may be viewed as a theory of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} groupoids
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong set
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
              in which there is an operation connecting any two parallel
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} paths
              by a 2-path:
            %pre
              %code Definition is_set A := forall (x y : A) (p q : x == y), p == q
            %p
              (There are numerous other equivalent definitions; see
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"} h-set
          %p
            Because this operation is defined internally, it acts on paths as well as points and implies that, for any two points of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} type of paths
            between them is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+space"} contractible
            as soon as it is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited"} inhabited
          %p
            Regarding types as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"}> type of types
            ). If we have chosen one particular universe as the universe of “small” things, then “setness” could be restricted to types belonging to that universe.
          %p
            Finally, skeletality is mostly meaningless in HoTT because paths are the only notion of (propositional)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            which we have to reason with. (There is also the notion of “definitional” equality, but as we cannot assert or prove statements of the form “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are definitionally equal” inside the theory, this is mostly not relevant for the purposes of setness.)
