          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            Formalization of sets in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (via
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-sets"} h-sets
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Egbert+Rijke"}> Egbert Rijke
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bas+Spitters"} Bas Spitters
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sets in homotopy type theory
              %strong 25
              5 “From type theory and homotopy theory to Univalent Foundations of Mathematics” (2015) 1172-1202
              %p
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129514000553"}> doi:10.1017/S0960129514000553
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1305.3835"} arXiv:1305.3835
          %h3#history History
          %p An early definition of “set” appears in
          %ul
            %li
              Bernard Bolzano,
              %em Paradoxien des Unendlichen
              (1847)
          %p where in section 4 it says in translation
          %blockquote
            %p There are wholes which, although they contain the same parts A, B, C, D, …, nevertheless present themselves as different when seen from our point of view or conception (this kind of difference we call ‘essential’), e.g. a complete and a broken glass viewed as a drinking vessel. [] A whole whose basic conception renders the arrangement of its parts a matter of indifference (and whose rearrangement therefore changes nothing essential from our point of view, if only that changes), I call a set.
        .revisedby
